Subject: [PATCH] Do not intercept same-origin .wasm fetches

Chrome refuses its compiled-code cache for a response a service
worker constructed, and this worker constructs one for every fetch
so it can add the isolation headers. Left to the network, the
engine's .wasm reaches the page as a plain response and a returning
visitor reuses the tiered code: the prompt comes about a second
sooner (docs/engine-execution.md, "The code cache and the shim").

Only the same-origin .wasm. It needs no resource policy header, while
a worker script's response must carry the embedder policy or the
engine's pthread workers lose cross-origin isolation.

---
--- a/coi-serviceworker.js
+++ b/coi-serviceworker.js
@@ -27,6 +27,17 @@
             return;
         }
 
+        // Do not intercept our own .wasm files. Chrome will not use its
+        // compiled-code cache for a response a service worker built, so
+        // intercepting them makes every visit recompile the engine.
+        // Same-origin files need no CORP header, so the network response
+        // is fine as is. Worker scripts are not exempt: they must carry
+        // COEP or the worker is not cross-origin isolated.
+        const url = new URL(r.url);
+        if (r.mode !== "navigate" && url.origin === self.location.origin && url.pathname.endsWith(".wasm")) {
+            return;
+        }
+
         const request = (coepCredentialless && r.mode === "no-cors")
             ? new Request(r, {
                 credentials: "omit",
